+D3 above proposes adding `r"^docs/tickets\.json$"` to `HARNESS_STATE`. **Decline
+it.** This is no longer an argument from reading: it was run twice against the
+real lifecycle in a throwaway project, with that one line as the only difference.
+
+**What the code does.** Line numbers are for `.claude/scripts/harness_lib.py`
+*with the pending close-hardening patch applied*:
+
+- `integrity()` (l.137, union at l.142-144) allows `scope ∪ META_ALLOW ∪
+  HARNESS_STATE` in a single `or`. Anything named in `HARNESS_STATE` is
+  structurally invisible to the close-time check — that is the list's purpose.
+- That check's own docstring (l.138-140) calls itself "the compensating control
+  for the documented Bash hole: out-of-band edits to protected files (the plan,
+  the hooks) fail the close."
+- `PROTECTED` cannot cover for it. `PROTECTED` is read only by the PreToolUse
+  guard, and `.claude/settings.json`'s only PreToolUse matchers are
+  `Edit|Write|NotebookEdit` and `TaskUpdate`. The Bash tool — which harness rule 2
+  makes the *required* way to drive the lifecycle — matches neither, and
+  `backend/tests/hooks/test_close_unattributed_claim_gap.py::test_no_pretooluse_hook_matches_bash_tool_calls`
+  already pins that. For `docs/tickets.json`, `integrity()` is therefore not *a*
+  control, it is the *only* one. D3 removes it.
+
+**Reproduction.** A session claims a ticket whose verify is
+`test -f src/done.txt`, never creates that file, rewrites its own ticket through
+Bash so the verify becomes `true`, and closes:
